WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.600
Why are prime-length unary strings

2
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.820
nonregular? This harder companion follows

3
00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:08.280
the tutorial’s requested Myhill Nerode

4
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.280
proof rather than replacing it

5
00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:12.080
with a different method. We

6
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:13.820
will use the number-theory theorem

7
00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.320
supplied in the question to

8
00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:16.880
construct a suffix for every

9
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:18.780
pair of prime-length prefixes.

10
00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.340
The supplied theorem says an

11
00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:23.480
arithmetic progression with positive coprime

12
00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.540
initial term and step contains

13
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:27.840
infinitely many primes. We need

14
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:29.300
that statement as a tool,

15
00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:30.600
not a proof of the

16
00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.480
theorem itself. Its coprimality hypothesis

17
00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:35.080
will come from choosing two

18
00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.220
distinct primes.

19
00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.060
Choose X to be the

20
00:00:38.060 --> 00:00:40.400
language itself, all unary words

21
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.740
of prime length. Take arbitrary

22
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.960
different members with prime lengths

23
00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.780
n and m. Distinct primes

24
00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:49.740
have greatest common divisor one,

25
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.800
so the progression starting at

26
00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.640
n with step m satisfies

27
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.080
the theorem’s hypotheses.

28
00:00:55.740 --> 00:00:57.880
There are infinitely many prime

29
00:00:57.880 --> 00:00:59.880
terms in that progression, so

30
00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:01.520
choose one with k at

31
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.660
least one. Define z to

32
00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:05.260
be k times m copies

33
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:07.220
of zero. Appending the same

34
00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:08.880
z to both prefixes will

35
00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.680
now produce lengths with deliberately

36
00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:12.380
different arithmetic structures.

37
00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.780
The word x z has

38
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:16.560
length n plus k m,

39
00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.020
which was chosen prime. Therefore

40
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.500
x z belongs to the

41
00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:22.700
prime-length language. This is the

42
00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:24.300
positive membership half of the

43
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.380
distinguishing argument.

44
00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:27.440
The word y z has

45
00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:29.040
length m plus k m,

46
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:30.540
equal to k plus one

47
00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:32.500
times m. Both factors are

48
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.820
at least two because k

49
00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.200
is positive and m is

50
00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:37.100
prime. Its length is therefore

51
00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:39.300
composite, so y z does

52
00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:40.600
not belong to the language.

53
00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:43.040
For an illustration, choose n

54
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.720
equal to two and m

55
00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:46.780
equal to three. Taking k

56
00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:47.940
equal to one gives a

57
00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:50.860
three-zero suffix, producing length five

58
00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.460
from the first prefix and

59
00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:53.740
length six from the second.

60
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.120
Five is prime while six

61
00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:57.920
is composite, but the theorem

62
00:01:57.920 --> 00:01:59.640
is what covers every arbitrary

63
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:00.560
prime pair.

64
00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.120
We have distinguished every two

65
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.380
distinct members of X. Since

66
00:02:05.380 --> 00:02:07.260
there are infinitely many primes,

67
00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.720
X is infinite, so the

68
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.700
language has infinite index. The

69
00:02:12.700 --> 00:02:15.020
Myhill Nerode theorem then implies

70
00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:16.220
it cannot be regular.

71
00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:18.980
Do not claim that testing

72
00:02:18.980 --> 00:02:21.240
several small prime lengths establishes

73
00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:22.900
the result. Nor does a

74
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.660
failed pumping attempt imply that

75
00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:26.360
the language is regular. This

76
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.780
proof works because it gives

77
00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:30.180
an arbitrary-pair suffix and verifies

78
00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:32.040
both membership outcomes under a

79
00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:32.660
stated theorem.

80
00:02:33.100 --> 00:02:34.260
Things to take note of.

81
00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.920
Separate the provided number-theory fact

82
00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:39.080
from the automata argument, choose

83
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:41.520
two distinct prime lengths, and

84
00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.220
use a positive progression index.

85
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:45.780
The same appended unary suffix

86
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.480
makes one length prime and

87
00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:49.360
the other composite, giving the

88
00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.820
required infinite-index contradiction.
